-- title:
--   The Hauptmodul λ lies in the level-four function field
-- statement:
--   The statement is closed: it has no variables or hypotheses. Let `lambdaInt` be the integral Laurent series obtained as the product of the series $q$ (the Hahn series `HahnSeries.single 1 1`), the eighth power of the power series `etaProd` regarded as a Laurent series, the image under `qExpand ℤ 4` of the sixteenth power of that same Laurent series, and the image under `qExpand ℤ 2` of the Laurent series attached to `dedekindEtaUnitInv` — the two `qExpand` operators performing the substitutions of the variable indicated by their indices. Let `lambdaModC ℚ` be its coefficientwise image under the canonical ring homomorphism $\mathbb Z \to \mathbb Q$, an element of the field $\mathbb Q((q))$ of rational Laurent series. The assertion is that `lambdaModC ℚ` belongs to `modularFunctionFieldFull 4`, the intermediate field of $\mathbb Q((q))$ over $\mathbb Q$ generated by the set of all series `qExpand ℚ d jq` with $d$ a nonzero divisor of $4$, i.e. by the three series $j(q)$, $j(q^{2})$, $j(q^{4})$ obtained from `jq` by the substitutions of index $1$, $2$ and $4$.
--
--   This is the membership form of the classical fact that the Hauptmodul of $X_0(4)$, with $q$-expansion $q\prod_{n\ge 1}(1-q^{n})^{8}(1-q^{4n})^{16}/\prod_{n\ge1}(1-q^{2n})^{24}$, is a rational function of $j(\tau)$, $j(2\tau)$ and $j(4\tau)$. It feeds the construction and the symmetry (swap and anharmonic) identities for the level-two modular polynomial data, and the computation of minimal polynomials of substituted Hauptmoduln over $\mathbb Q(\lambda)$.
